11. Why yield matters for ASML, Lam and Applied Materials too

Yield also affects process-tool vendors.

If a new process requires tighter lithography accuracy, better etch profiles or more precise films to achieve target yield, the customer may need higher-performance equipment or more process steps.

That can increase equipment intensity.

Examples:

ASML: better patterning and overlay reduce lithography-driven defects.

Lam Research: more precise etch can improve feature shape and reduce damage.

Applied Materials: better deposition and materials engineering can reduce film variation and defects.

KLA: inspection and metrology identify where the process is drifting.

The whole equipment ecosystem ultimately exists to help customers manufacture good chips consistently.

12. Yield and chiplets

Chiplets are one response to the yield problem.

Instead of building one enormous monolithic die, a designer can sometimes split a system into several smaller dies and connect them in a package.

Why can this help?

Smaller dies generally have higher yield because each die occupies less wafer area and is less likely to contain a random defect.

A simplified example:

One very large monolithic die may have poor yield.

Four smaller dies may each yield better, and the system can be assembled from good dies.

This does not make chiplets automatically cheaper.

Advanced packaging adds cost, interconnect complexity, power challenges and its own yield risk.

But chiplets let designers trade some front-end die-yield difficulty for packaging complexity.

We will go much deeper into this when we reach advanced packaging.

13. Known-good die

This leads to an important packaging term: known-good die.

A known-good die is a die that has already been tested and shown to function before being combined with other expensive components.

Imagine assembling an advanced AI package containing:

- one expensive compute die;
- several HBM stacks;
- an interposer or advanced substrate;
- complex packaging steps.

If one component is bad, it can destroy the value of the entire package.

So testing components before assembly becomes extremely important.

As AI packages become more expensive, packaging and test yield become increasingly strategic.

14. Yield and HBM

HBM illustrates multiplicative yield risk.

An HBM stack combines multiple memory dies vertically.

If several components must all work together, one bad component can reduce the yield of the final stack.

This makes testing, known-good-die strategies, bonding quality and packaging process control critical.

The same principle will matter when we later study CoWoS and other advanced AI packaging systems.

AI systems are moving toward increasingly expensive multi-component packages.

That means yield is no longer only a front-end wafer issue.

It extends through memory stacking, packaging and final system assembly.

